<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Entitlements applied to every binary Tauri signs in this bundle (see config/generate.ts
  bundle.macOS.entitlements — this file is REFERENCED there, not generated).

  disable-library-validation is load-bearing, not boilerplate. Signing the bundle (SP2.5 fix for the
  "app está danificado" dialog) makes Tauri sign with `--options runtime` = HARDENED RUNTIME, and
  hardened runtime turns on LIBRARY VALIDATION: a process may only dlopen code signed by the same
  Team ID. The daemon sidecar loads a native module at runtime —
  `daemon-runtime/node_modules/@libsql/darwin-arm64/index.node` — which carries its own, separate
  ad-hoc signature. Two ad-hoc signatures are two identities (neither has a Team ID), so macOS
  refused the load:

    dlopen(.../index.node): code signature not valid for use in process:
    mapping process and mapped file (non-platform) have different Team IDs

  Measured on the shipped v0.1.2 (2026-08-07): the daemon died at boot, the shell's 60s readiness
  gate fired, and the user saw only "um ou mais serviços não responderam". Verified fix: re-signing
  that binary with this entitlement makes the daemon boot and answer GET /v1/health with 200.

  This stays necessary after a real Developer ID arrives — a notarized app still runs the hardened
  runtime, and the prebuilt .node still is not ours to sign as a team member. The alternative would
  be signing every native prebuild with the same identity at build time; that is more moving parts
  for the same outcome.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
